          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Aristotle</strong><br>\nAristotle was born in 384 BC in Stagira, a small town on the northeastern coast of Greece. His father was the personal physician to King Amyntas of Macedon, which allowed Aristotle to associate with the Macedonian court. This connection later provided him with the opportunity to tutor Alexander the Great. Aristotle's early life played a significant role in shaping his philosophical outlook.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aristotle's Education</strong><br>\nAt the age of seventeen or eighteen, Aristotle moved to Athens to continue his education at Plato's Academy. He stayed there for nearly twenty years, not just as a student but also as a researcher and teacher. Plato's influence on Aristotle was profound, although Aristotle would later challenge many of Plato's theories, including the famous Theory of Forms.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding the Lyceum</strong><br>\nAfter Plato's death, Aristotle left Athens and eventually set up his own school in 335 BC, called the Lyceum. Here, Aristotle conducted courses and extensive research, especially into the realm of natural sciences. His method of systematic observation and collection of data laid the groundwork for modern empirical research.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
