          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Politics and Ethics</strong><br>\nIn his work \"Politics,\" Aristotle examined the nature of the state, citizenship, and government. He preferred a constitutional government. His \"Nicomachean Ethics\" outlines his views on virtue ethics, focusing on personal virtue and character, proposing that a virtuous life is one of rational activity in accordance with virtue.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aristotle's Legacy</strong><br>\nAristotle's ideas have a profound impact on Western thought. His works were rediscovered in the Middle Ages and became a cornerstone of medieval philosophy and theology. His logic remained unchallenged until the 19th century, and his metaphysics influenced an array of philosophical discussions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aristotle's Influence on Science</strong><br>\nAristotle's classifications of plants and animals based on structural characteristics laid the foundation for scientific taxonomy. Although some of his scientific ideas were later proven incorrect, his approach to systematic observation and categorization has been a lasting contribution to the scientific method.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
